lo":25.134805,"d":889,"f":["wc"]},{"n":"phone center","g":"essentials","k":"mobile_phone","la":35.33931,"lo":25.13329,"d":890,"ad":"5 Πλατεία Ελευθερίου Βενιζέλου"},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":35.339278,"lo":25.13322,"d":897,"f":["wc"]},{"n":"Discount","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":35.340401,"lo":25.151415,"d":901,"f":["wc"]},{"n":"Kapa Change","g":"essentials","k":"bureau_de_change","la":35.339274,"lo":25.133118,"d":905,"w":"https://www.kapachange.gr/","ad":"11 Πλατεία Ελευθερίου Βενιζέλου"},{"n":"Pharmacy","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":35.336871,"lo":25.135261,"d":915,"ad":"14"},{"n":"Piraeus Bank","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":35.337885,"lo":25.133716,"d":942},{"n":"Pharmacy","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":35.338814,"lo":25.132874,"d":949},{"n":"Piraeus Bank","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":35.338951,"lo":25.13256,"d":967},{"n":"Ottoman Crete","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.333333,"lo":25.133333,"d":1331,"ds":"The island of Crete (Ottoman Turkish: كریت, romanized: Girit) was declared an Ottoman province (eyalet) in 1646, after the Turks conquered the western part of the island as part of the Cretan War, but the Venetians maintained their hold on the capital Candia, until 1669, when Francesco Morosini surrendered the keys of the town.","wpu":"Ottoman Crete","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Flag_of_the_Ottoman_Empire_%281844%E2%80%931922%29.svg/960px-Flag_of_the_Ottoman_Empire_%281844%E2%80%931922%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kingdom of Candia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.316667,"lo":25.133333,"d":3031,"ds":"The Realm or Kingdom of Candia or Duchy of Candia was the official name of Crete during the island's period as an overseas territory of the Republic of Venice, from the initial Venetian conquest in 1205–1212 to its fall to the Ottoman Empire during the Cretan War (1645–1669). The island was at the time and up to the early modern era commonly known as Candia after its capital, Candia or Chandax (modern Heraklion).","wpu":"Kingdom of Candia","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5d/MBoschini_Il_regno_tutto_di_Candia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Knossos","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.298056,"lo":25.163056,"d":5350,"ds":"Knossos (; Ancient Greek: Κνωσσός, romanized: Knōssós; Greek: Κνωσός, romanized: Knōsós; Linear B: 𐀒𐀜𐀰 Ko-no-so) is an archaeological site and ancient urban centre in Crete, Greece. It is located within the southern periphery of Heraklion, about 5 km (3.1 mi) south of the city's historic centre.","wpu":"Knossos","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Crete_integrated_map-en.svg/960px-Crete_integrated_map-en.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Anemospilia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":35.255444,"lo":25.144528,"d":9738,"ds":"Anemospilia (Greek: τα Ανεμόσπηλια) is the archaeological site of an ancient Minoan temple on Crete.","wpu":"Anemospilia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Anemospilia.jpg/960px-Anemospilia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"The Heraklion Archaeological Museum is a museum located in Heraklion on Crete. It is one of the largest museums in Greece, and the best in the world for Minoan art, as it contains by far the most important and complete collection of artefacts of the Minoan civilization of Crete. It is normally referred to scholarship in English as \"AMH\" (for \"Archaeological Museum of Heraklion\"), a form still sometimes used by the museum in itself.","wpu":"Heraklion Archaeological Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Stierspringer-Elfenbeinstatuette_Knossos_01.jpg/960px-Stierspringer-Elfenbeinstatuette_Knossos_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}